      "questionId": "q8",
      "maxMarks": 16,
      "answer": "The working memory model (Baddeley & Hitch, 1974) proposes a multi-component system for short-term processing. It includes the central executive (attentional control), the phonological loop (verbal/acoustic information), the visuospatial sketchpad (visual/spatial information), and the episodic buffer (added in 2000, integrates information). Evaluation includes supporting evidence: KF case study (Shallice & Warrington, 1970) shows intact visual but impaired verbal STM, supporting separate stores; dual-task paradigm studies (e.g., Baddeley et al., 1984) show interference between tasks using same component, supporting distinct stores; word-length effect (Baddeley et al., 1984) supports phonological loop's articulatory rehearsal; brain imaging (e.g., Smith et al., 1995) shows activation in relevant areas. Criticisms: vague role of central executive (little explanation of how it works); model focuses only on STM/short-term processing, not long-term memory; episodic buffer added later, suggesting incomplete original model.",
      "acceptableAlternatives": [
        "Any valid supporting or contradicting evidence accepted: KF (intact visual but impaired verbal STM), dual-task paradigm studies, word-length effect, episodic buffer added in 2000, brain imaging of phonological loop",
        "Criticisms: vague role of central executive, model focuses only on STM/short-term processing"
      ],
      "commonMistakes": [
        "describing components without evaluation",
        "confusing WMM with multi-store model",
        "asserting evidence without naming/summarising the study",
        "unbalanced answer (all support or all criticism)"
      ],
